  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They held the lead for three innings and gave it back in one. That's the game that's wearing this team down.",
    "what_this_means": "Wilkinson couldn't get out of the fourth. He lasted 3.2 innings, gave up four runs, and when he left, the deficit was already two. The bullpen did what it had to. Brubaker, Hentges, and Harris combined for 3.2 scoreless innings, no runner making it matter. But the bats had already checked out by then. Cox went 2-for-4 with a home run and an RBI. Eldridge added another hit and drove in a run. Two runners on base converted to runs, which is something. But in a game decided by one, something is not enough. They scored in the first and held that lead through the third. By the fourth, it was gone. Boston added three that inning and the Giants never got close again. This is what inconsistency looks like in real time. Brief moments of control followed by the kind of damage that can't be undone.",
    "what_to_watch": "Chase Burns is 2.51 ERA, and Cincinnati's offense is batting .227 as a team. Tonight the matchup favors San Francisco's lineup, such as it is.",
